<h2>Color Palette: The Deliberate Restraint</h2>
<p>A true Minimalist palette is built around one dominant neutral — usually white, off-white, or a warm light grey — with a single contrast tone, often black or a deep charcoal, used sparingly for definition (a door frame, a light fixture, cabinet hardware). Color, when it appears at all, shows up through a single material choice rather than paint: the natural tone of a wood floor, the black of a matte tap, a single deliberate red object used exactly once as a focal point.</p>
<h3>Common palette mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Using more than one accent tone:</strong> Immediately reads as Contemporary or Scandinavian, not Minimalist.</li>
  <li><strong>Cold white without warm-white lighting balance:</strong> Produces a sterile, clinical feel rather than calm restraint.</li>
  <li><strong>Treating "neutral" as an excuse to skip color decisions entirely:</strong> A true Minimalist palette is still a deliberate choice, not a default.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Materials: Fewer Choices, Higher Standard</h2>
<p>Because Minimalist interiors have nowhere to hide a poor material choice — there's no busy pattern or heavy trim to distract the eye — each material used has to genuinely perform, both visually and physically.</p>
