<h2>Who Is the Ideal Minimalist Customer?</h2>
<p>Young professionals and first-time buyers who want a considered home without heavy furniture investment, busy dual-income families who genuinely don't have time for high-maintenance décor, and downsizers moving from a larger, more cluttered home who specifically want less to manage.</p>
<h2>Advantages</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lower long-term maintenance:</strong> fewer surfaces and objects to clean and manage.</li>
  <li><strong>Makes small spaces feel larger:</strong> restrained furniture and consistent color reduce the visual clutter that shrinks a room's perceived size.</li>
  <li><strong>Timeless, not trend-dependent:</strong> a well-executed Minimalist interior doesn't visibly date the way heavily styled interiors can.</li>
  <li><strong>Often lower material cost:</strong> fewer finishes and less applied detail, though each finish used should be higher quality.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Disadvantages</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Unforgiving of poor planning:</strong> storage and layout mistakes are highly visible with nowhere to hide.</li>
  <li><strong>Can feel cold if executed without warmth:</strong> needs deliberate texture and lighting choices to avoid a clinical feel.</li>
  <li><strong>Requires discipline from occupants:</strong> the look degrades quickly if new objects aren't managed with the same restraint used to design it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Maintenance Level</h2>
<p>Low</p>
<p>Fewer surfaces and objects genuinely mean less cleaning time. The one caveat: handle-less, high-gloss, or dark matte surfaces show fingerprints and dust more visibly than textured or lighter finishes, so factor a slightly higher wiping frequency into your expectations if you choose those finishes.</p>
